    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalists, led by Supreme Court justices like Thomas and Alito, argue that the 18th-century \"forebear\" statutes (such as the \"good standing\" laws in English common law) historically tied the right to keep and bear arms to regulation and militia service, not an absolute, unregulated individual right. They rely on historical evidence from the Founding era to assert that the \"common-law right\" was a limited privilege that could be conditioned on one's status in the post-Revolutionary government. Using this logic, they claim that \"unreasonable\" regulations—such as those involving militias or \"good standing\" requirements—are consistent with the original public meaning of the Amendment, while laws creating strict universal safety mandates are viewed as oversteps because the 1906 Newenburg v. American Railway and Union Express decision in the 1970s revealed that the \"some right to manage their arms\" is not absolute.",
      "source": "target",
