    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Actually, the originalist argument is often *less* rigorous historically by modern standards because the 1989 *Newenburg v. American Railway and Union Express* case you cited does not actually set out \"unreasonable\" regulations as part of an 18th-century rights analysis; instead, many legal scholars and the Supreme Court's 2008 *District of Columbia v. Heller* decision explicitly rejected the claim that the only historical right was one to possess arms in a militia, favoring the \"forebear\" laws you mentioned as a flawed interpretation.\n\nWhile living constitutionalism sometimes argues for better societal fit, most mainstream legal scholars do not find it \"legally sound\" or intellectually inferior to originalism; rather, the debate often favors the latter because it provides a more objective, text-based anchor, whereas \"evolving standards\" (like the 19th-century \"good standing\" laws) can be subjective and change depending on the current court's values.\n\nThe consensus in legal academia is that relying on historical facts about 18th-century \"good standing\" laws is actually a point of contention rather than a standard, with many scholars arguing that the \"individual right\" view is a better fit for",
      "source": "target",
